    {
      "id": "w6hl1j8",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4. PERCENTAGE INCREASE & DECREASE"
    },
    {
      "id": "dvnoeli",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "Increase Percentage:<br></br>(Increase / Original) × 100",
        "Decrease Percentage:<br></br>(Decrease / Original) × 100<br></br>",
        "Example:<br></br>Price increases from ₹200 to ₹250.<br></br>Increase = 50<br></br>(50/200) × 100 = 25%<br></br>Increase = 25%"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "bqs3r9w",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5. SUCCESSIVE PERCENTAGE CHANGE"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "7suex98",
      "content": "When two percentage changes occur successively:"
    },
    {
      "id": "d051qkg",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "Net % = a + b + (ab/100)",
        "Use negative sign for decrease.<br></br>Example:<br></br>Increase by 10% and then decrease by 10%.<br></br>Net = 10 – 10 – (100/100)<br></br>Net decrease = 1%"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Parameter",
        "Percentage Increase",
        "Percentage Decrease"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Numerator Value",
          "Final Value - Original Value",
          "Original Value - Final Value"
        ],
        [
          "Multiplying Factor",
          "1 + (r / 100)",
          "1 - (r / 100)"
        ],
        [
          "Typical Application",
          "Price appreciation, population growth, inflation",
          "Discounts, depreciation, value reduction"
        ]
      ],
      "id": "bfc2ksd"
    },
    {
      "id": "b2nejps",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "6. PERCENTAGE OF A QUANTITY"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "javeu41",
      "content": "Formula:"
    },
    {
      "id": "ismywv2",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "x% of y = (x/100) × y<br></br>Example:<br></br>15% of 400 = (15/100) × 400 = 60"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ii3cyjo",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "7. REVERSE PERCENTAGE"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "td1uhmu",
      "content": "Example:<br></br>If 20% of a number is 50, find the number.<br></br>(20/100) × x = 50<br></br>x = 250"
    },
    {
      "id": "vq5zh5k",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "8. SOLVED EXAMPLES"
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "Memorize standard fractional equivalents (e.g., 1/7 = 14.28%, 1/8 = 12.5%) to solve calculations faster in time-bound exams.",
        "Apply the net effect formula a + b + (ab / 100) for successive percentage changes instead of step-by-step calculations.",
        "Pay close attention to the base value in reverse percentage problems to avoid taking percentage of the modified amount."
      ],
      "id": "sbv9k4x"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "jgywdfk",
      "content": "<strong>Example 1:</strong><br></br>A student scored 360 marks out of 450. Find the percentage.<br></br>(360/450) × 100 = 80%<br></br><br></br><strong>Example 2:</strong><br></br>Population of a town is 40,000. It increases by 5%.<br></br>Increase = 2,000<br></br>New population = 42,000"
    },
    {
      "id": "doqv9pk",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "9. PRACTICE QUESTIONS"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "thezciu",
      "content": "Q1. What is 25% of 640?<br></br>A. 120   B. 140   C. 160   D. 180<br></br><br></br>Q2. Price decreases from ₹500 to ₹450. Find the percentage decrease.<br></br>A. 8%   B. 9%   C. 10%   D. 12%<br></br><br></br>Q3. A number is increased by 20% and then decreased by 10%. Find the net change.<br></br>A. 8% increase   B. 10% decrease   C. No change   D. 2% increase"
    },
